          "children": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Shared Histories, Divergent Paths, and Caribbean Connections",
                  "italic": true,
                  "bold": true
                }
              ],
              "align": "center"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Cuba and Puerto Rico have long occupied interconnected positions within the political, economic, and cultural history of the Caribbean. Their relationship extends beyond geographical proximity or the movement of people between two islands. Spanish colonialism, African slavery, anticolonial struggles, United States expansion, and changing forms of Caribbean mobility created historical connections that transcended the boundaries of each territory. Yet these shared experiences did not produce identical societies or political trajectories. Their histories are intertwined precisely because the relationship between them has been shaped by both convergence and divergence."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": ""
        }
      ]
