                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "align": "center",
              "children": [
                {
                  "bold": true,
                  "text": "Colonial Connections and Anticolonial Solidarities"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "column",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Under Spanish colonial rule, Cuba and Puerto Rico developed within overlapping imperial structures. Both experienced plantation expansion, forced African labor, and the racial hierarchies of slavery, although differences in economic organization, demographic composition, and political circumstances shaped their respective trajectories. The nineteenth century brought struggles over abolition, citizenship, colonial authority, and political sovereignty that generated connections among intellectuals, activists, and revolutionary organizations across the Caribbean."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "These relationships acquired particular significance in the anticolonial movements of the late nineteenth century. The Grito de Lares in Puerto Rico and the Grito de Yara in Cuba, both in 1868, emerged from distinct local circumstances but reflected challenges to the continuation of Spanish colonial rule. Figures such as Ramón Emeterio Betances, Eugenio María de Hostos, and José Martí participated in wider intellectual and political networks through which the liberation of one island could be understood in relation to the future of the other."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "These networks also extended beyond the Caribbean. Cuban and Puerto Rican activists organized political clubs and circulated newspapers in cities such as New York, creating spaces where national independence projects encountered broader arguments about race, sovereignty, and Antillean solidarity. Their cooperation was historically significant without eliminating differences in political priorities or social position."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": ""
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "grid",
      "layout": [
        4,
        2
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "column",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "The events of 1898 represented a turning point for both societies. The Spanish-American War ended Spanish colonial rule in Cuba and Puerto Rico, but the resulting political arrangements differed substantially. Puerto Rico became a territory of the United States, while Cuba, following a period of American military occupation, established a republic in 1902 under conditions that preserved significant possibilities for U.S. intervention."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "These arrangements transformed the relationship between the islands. Puerto Rico's incorporation into the United States territorial system, including the extension of U.S. citizenship to Puerto Ricans in 1917, produced a distinct framework of political membership and mobility. Cuba's republican institutions developed under continuing U.S. economic and political influence, while debates over national sovereignty remained central to its political life."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "The significance of 1898 therefore lies not only in the end of Spanish rule, but also in the emergence of different relationships with a new imperial power. As Lillian Guerra argues in “Passive Puerto Rico and Revolutionary Cuba? Myths, Realities, and the Optics of History,” representations of Cuba as inherently revolutionary and Puerto Rico as politically passive obscured the different strategies through which people in both societies challenged and negotiated imperial power. Their divergent political arrangements should not be mistaken for an absence of shared historical concerns."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
