            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "bold": true,
                  "text": "The Cuban Revolution and the Reconfiguration of Caribbean Relations"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "column",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "The Cuban Revolution of 1959 introduced another major transformation in the relationship between the two islands. In the context of the Cold War, Cuba and Puerto Rico became associated with contrasting political and economic projects. Revolutionary Cuba pursued socialist transformation and increasingly confrontational relations with the United States, while Puerto Rico's government promoted an alternative model of development within its existing political relationship with Washington."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "These differences were neither inevitable nor universally accepted. Silvia Álvarez Curbelo's examination of Governor Luis Muñoz Marín demonstrates how his initial response to the Cuban Revolution gave way to growing opposition as its political direction changed. Puerto Rico thus became not simply a neighboring territory but also a point of comparison in debates about revolution, development, democracy, and the future of Caribbean societies."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Migration intensified these connections while creating new divisions. Cuban arrivals during the 1960s and 1970s developed family networks, professional activities, and cultural institutions in Puerto Rico. Their experiences brought Cuban political disagreements into a society already engaged in its own debates about sovereignty, national identity, and relations with the United States."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "At the same time, solidarity with the Cuban Revolution and projects of Puerto Rican self-determination generated other forms of political identification and collaboration. These relationships remind us that Cuban–Puerto Rican connections cannot be represented exclusively through the experiences or institutions of anticommunist exile."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": ""
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "grid",
      "layout": [
        4,
        2
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "column",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Political divergence did not eliminate the circulation of cultural practices, intellectual exchanges, or the formation of relationships across the islands and their diasporas. Music, literature, journalism, educational institutions, and community organizations continued to provide spaces through which Cubans and Puerto Ricans encountered and reinterpreted one another's experiences."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "These connections were not confined to direct movement between Cuba and Puerto Rico. Diasporic communities elsewhere, particularly in the United States, also participated in shaping relationships between the two societies. Laura Lomas's research on the magazine "
                },
                {
                  "text": "Areíto",
                  "italic": true
                },
                {
                  "text": ", for example, examines collaborations among Cuban and Puerto Rican migrant intellectuals during the 1970s and early 1980s, connecting cultural production with political debates over decolonization and national self-determination."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Francisco Scarano further identifies cultural convergences that continued despite the islands' divergent Cold War trajectories. Such exchanges challenge interpretations that treat political separation as an end to the historical relationship between Cuba and Puerto Rico."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "From this perspective, Cuban migration to Puerto Rico belongs to a wider history of Caribbean connections rather than an isolated movement from one national community into another. The institutions, memories, and cultural practices created by Cuban migrants developed within a Puerto Rican society whose own history had long been connected to Cuba."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "These intertwined histories establish the context for the audiovisual testimonies, newspapers, and institutional materials examined in this project. They also raise questions about the ways historical relationships are remembered: which connections become central to community narratives, which political differences shape the preservation of those narratives, and which experiences remain outside the most visible institutional records."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
